# Somnibeat: an overnight sleep report from heartbeat intervals

**Research prototype · not a diagnostic tool.**
Live demo: https://handue.github.io/somnibeat/ · Code: https://github.com/handue/somnibeat

Every number in this document is filled in from `results/*.json` (v1) and
`results/v2/*.json` (v2) by `scripts/render_docs.py`; none is typed by hand.

## 1. Problem and who it is for

Sleep is measured properly with polysomnography (PSG): a night in a sleep lab
with EEG, airflow and oxygen sensors. It is expensive, has waiting lists, and
measures one unusual night. Many people already have a much simpler signal
from a night of sleep: the time between heartbeats (RR intervals), recorded by
a Holter monitor, a hospital ECG or a chest strap. Heart rate and its
variability change with sleep stage and with breathing pauses, so the RR
series carries some sleep information.

Somnibeat asks a narrow question: **from RR intervals alone, how well can we
estimate the numbers a sleep report starts with (total sleep time, sleep
efficiency, wake bouts, minutes with suspected apnea), and how wrong can that
estimate be?** The second half of the question is the point of the project:
every number in the report is shown next to its measured error on recordings
the model never saw.

Intended users are researchers and clinicians who already have overnight RR
data and want a quick, transparent first look, and students of sleep
physiology. It is not intended for self-diagnosis.

## 2. Method

There are two model versions. **v1** is the first, pre-registered model.
**v2** is a second version chosen on training data only under a protocol
written before any v2 experiment ([`docs/v2_protocol.md`](v2_protocol.md)).
The demo runs v2 by default; v1 remains available.

**Data.** Five public PhysioNet databases, all with ECG and expert annotations:

| Database | Role | Size used | Population |
|---|---|---|---|
| CAP Sleep Database | stage training | v1: {{v:sleep_wake/data/train/cap/records}} records; v2: {{len:v2_selection_cap106/records/cap}} records | v1: 15 healthy controls and patients with sleep disorders (insomnia, PLM, narcolepsy, RBD, sleep-disordered breathing, bruxism); v2 adds the remaining usable records, including nocturnal frontal lobe epilepsy |
| MIT-BIH Polysomnographic Database | stage training | {{v:sleep_wake/data/train/slpdb/records}} records, {{v:sleep_wake/data/train/slpdb/subjects}} subjects | men evaluated for sleep apnea, all male |
| Apnea-ECG | apnea training | {{v:apnea/data/train/apnea-ecg/records}} records, {{v:apnea/data/train/apnea-ecg/subjects}} subjects | per-minute apnea labels |
| UCDDB (St. Vincent's / UCD) | v1 external test; v2 second look | {{v:sleep_wake/data/test/ucddb/records}} records | adults with suspected sleep-disordered breathing (21 men, 4 women) |
| HMC Sleep Staging Database (Haaglanden Medisch Centrum) | v2 external test only | {{v:v2_test_results/test_sets/hmc/records_all}} records | patients referred for PSG for a range of sleep disorders; {{v:v2_hmc_check/summary/demographics_aggregated/male}} men and {{v:v2_hmc_check/summary/demographics_aggregated/female}} women, mean age {{v:v2_hmc_check/summary/demographics_aggregated/Age/mean:1}} ± {{v:v2_hmc_check/summary/demographics_aggregated/Age/std:1}} (as stated on the dataset page and in its demographics file; no age range or ethnicity given); AASM scoring |

The first four are licensed under the Open Data Commons Attribution License
v1.0, HMC under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0.

Before any modeling, `scripts/check_data.py` read the facts the plan relied on
from the files themselves (sampling rates, channel names, 30 s epochs,
alignment of stages, events and ECG, label definitions). All
{{v:data_check/n_checks}} v1 checks pass. Two surprises were resolved and
documented: an undocumented UCDDB stage code 8 (dropped as unscored), and a
difference in apnea labels (Apnea-ECG marks minutes containing apnea or
hypopnea; for UCDDB we mark minutes overlapped by any scored apnea or
hypopnea event). HMC was checked the same way before any prediction
(`results/v2/hmc_check.json`; {{v:v2_hmc_check/n_failed}} of {{v:v2_hmc_check/n_checks}} checks
fail now). In the first run one check failed: it flagged two recordings
whose ECG is mostly flat or clipped, a property of those recordings that led
to the coverage rule in section 3.2. That check now expects exactly those two
recordings and fails if any other recording falls outside the beat-rate range.

**ECG to RR.** An energy-envelope R-peak detector (Pan-Tompkins family) runs
at each database's native sampling rate. Against the MIT-BIH beat
annotations it finds {{pct:rpeak_validation/datasets/slpdb/pooled_sensitivity:2}}% of beats with
{{pct:rpeak_validation/datasets/slpdb/pooled_ppv:2}}% positive predictive value. RR intervals outside 300-2000 ms, or more
than 30 % away from the local median, are marked invalid and never
interpolated ([`docs/rr_rules.md`](rr_rules.md)). The detector and RR rules
are the same in v1 and v2.

**Features.** For each 30 s epoch (60 s for apnea): mean RR, SDNN, RMSSD,
pNN50 and coefficient of variation over 30 s, 150 s and 300 s windows;
Lomb-Scargle band powers, LF/HF and HF peak frequency; DFA alpha1. Each
feature is also robustly z-scored within the night, differenced against
neighbouring epochs and smoothed, plus time since the start of the night
({{v:v2_selection_cap106/n_v1_features}} features). v2 adds {{v:v2_selection_cap106/n_extra_features}} longer-context features (5-10 minute
windows and cyclic variation of heart rate).

**Models.** v1: gradient boosting (scikit-learn
`HistGradientBoostingClassifier`) and logistic regression, gradient boosting
primary; no deep learning. v2 staging: `{{v:v2_v2_selection/staging/chosen/name}}`, that is
gradient boosting with balanced class weights, the extra features, and a
hidden Markov model (Viterbi decoding) over the whole night whose transition
probabilities come from the training labels. v2 apnea:
`{{v:v2_v2_selection/apnea/chosen/name}}`, the mean of gradient boosting and logistic regression
probabilities, trained on Apnea-ECG. Stages map to Wake, REM,
light = S1 + S2 (N1 + N2) and deep = S3 + S4 (N3). Artifact or indeterminate
epochs are dropped.

**Same code in the browser.** The demo reimplements RR cleaning, every
feature, tree inference, the v2 smoothing and the report in JavaScript.
Parity tests check both versions against the Python reference: for v1,
features agree to about 1e-9 relative, class probabilities to about 1e-16 on
non-boundary rows, and per-epoch labels and night metrics are identical on
every sample night; for v2, per-epoch labels are identical and night metrics
and probabilities agree to about 1e-9 on every sample night.

## 3. Evaluation

### 3.1 v1: first look at UCDDB

The protocol was written ([`docs/evaluation.md`](evaluation.md)) and the
evaluation code tested before any model was trained, and a five-lens
adversarial review of the pipeline ran before the first test-set number was
computed ([`docs/review_2026-11-19.md`](review_2026-11-19.md)). It found, among
other things, that the feature cache was missing a training database because a
download had not finished. That would have silently changed every result. It
was fixed before the single test run.

- **Splits by subject.** Stage models train on CAP + MIT-BIH, apnea on
  Apnea-ECG; everything is tested on UCDDB. Cross-validation folds keep
  repeated recordings of one person together.
- **Thresholds on training data only.** The sleep/wake and apnea thresholds
  maximise epoch kappa on out-of-fold training predictions.
- **One look at the test set.** UCDDB was predicted once (git tag
  `v1-results`). The primary model (gradient boosting) was fixed by internal
  cross-validation before test numbers were read.
- **Baselines** on the same epochs: (a) asleep from lights-off; (b) the
  training majority class. For sleep/wake these two are identical (the
  majority class is sleep).
- **Statistics.** Record-level bootstrap ({{v:sleep_wake/data/test/ucddb/records}} records, 2000 resamples) for
  95 % CIs; Bland-Altman bias and limits of agreement.

| Metric | Gradient boosting (primary) | Logistic regression | Asleep from lights-off |
|---|---|---|---|
| Total sleep time MAE (min) | {{pm:sleep_wake/models/gradient_boosting/external_ucddb/metrics/tst_mae_min:1}} | {{pm:sleep_wake/models/logistic_regression/external_ucddb/metrics/tst_mae_min:1}} | {{p:sleep_wake/baselines/asleep_from_lights_off/metrics/tst_mae_min:1}} |
| Total sleep time bias (min) | {{sgn:sleep_wake/models/gradient_boosting/external_ucddb/metrics/tst_bias_min:1}} ({{ci:sleep_wake/models/gradient_boosting/external_ucddb/metrics/tst_bias_min:1}}) | {{sgn:sleep_wake/models/logistic_regression/external_ucddb/metrics/tst_bias_min:1}} | {{sgn:sleep_wake/baselines/asleep_from_lights_off/metrics/tst_bias_min:1}} |
